&lt;h2 id="so-what-exactly-is-gits-remote"&gt;So, What Exactly Is Git&amp;rsquo;s Remote?
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;We&amp;rsquo;ve mentioned before that Git uses &lt;em&gt;remotes&lt;/em&gt; to enable collaboration with others. We&amp;rsquo;ve even already covered a few commands for interacting with remotes. But how exactly does Git implement this functionality? What are its characteristics? What do we need to watch out for when collaborating on development with others? Let&amp;rsquo;s go through it step by step. First, let&amp;rsquo;s systematically cover what a &lt;em&gt;remote&lt;/em&gt; really is.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>(Maybe) a Git Tutorial? Part Two</title><link>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/shell_note/git_how/git_2/</link><pubDate>Thu, 14 Aug 2025 16:49:16 +0800</pubDate><guid>https://a-moment096.github.io/en/posts/shell_note/git_how/git_2/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://a-moment096.github.io/images/Tatara-Kogasa.jpg" alt="Featured image of post (Maybe) a Git Tutorial? Part Two" /&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The previous section covered how you&amp;rsquo;d normally use Git for single-branch repository management. This section is all about how Git handles multi-branch collaboration!&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
