&lt;h2 id="git-a-name-both-familiar-and-strange"&gt;Git, a Name Both Familiar and Strange&amp;hellip;
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;Maybe it&amp;rsquo;s just the environment I&amp;rsquo;m in, but a lot of people around me don&amp;rsquo;t know what Git is. They&amp;rsquo;ve all heard of &lt;em&gt;GitHub&lt;/em&gt;, but most only know that there are a ton of programs and programmers on it. That&amp;rsquo;s not exactly wrong, but it&amp;rsquo;s not quite accurate either; and when I say I use &lt;em&gt;Git&lt;/em&gt;, some people conflate &lt;em&gt;Git&lt;/em&gt; with &lt;em&gt;GitHub&lt;/em&gt;; many think Git is very complicated, and by extension think GitHub is also very complicated&amp;hellip; So, I&amp;rsquo;d like to share my understanding of Git and GitHub, and talk about what both Git and GitHub actually are.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>